=== FINDING ===
`umem --core <core> --exe <bin> findleaks` EXITS 0 AND PRINTS NOTHING.

Control in the same run, same binary, same allocations, same command: the
live-pid path printed a 597-byte report listing 200 outstanding buffers in 1
class.  The core path printed 0 bytes and returned success.  Exiting 0 with
an empty report is indistinguishable, to a script or a human, from "no
leaks found" -- so this is worse than an error.

Mechanism: tools/gdb/umem_gdb.py implements every command by making inferior
calls -- it fopen()s a temp file IN THE TARGET and calls umem_findleaks()
there (see _call_library()).  A core file is not a process; there is nothing
to execute those calls.  gdb declines, umem_gdb.py produces no output, and
tools/umem reports the debugger's success.

Reproduce: scripts/ec2/core_mode_probe.sh (after a build).
Host: intel-lo@surface, c7i.2xlarge, x86_64, Amazon Linux 2023, gdb 14.2.

Scope note: fixing this needs a passive core reader (read allocator
structures out of the core's memory image instead of calling into it), which
is Phase 3 of docs/plans/2026-09-21-production-readiness.md.  This
workstream withdraws the claim in umem(1), umem_debugging(7) and README.md
rather than leaving a documented command that silently reports nothing.
